WHY THIS WORKS
- acknowledges the loss specifically, briefly, then becomes useful
- arc 2 does no qualification at all because the loss happened days ago
- never implies stress caused the miscarriage

CONVERSATION 1: two losses, six months ago and last year

Lead: I've had two miscarriages now, the last one six months ago. Everything they've tested has come back normal and I don't understand what's wrong with me.

Sonia: Two losses and no explanation is a heavy thing to be carrying, and the not knowing makes it heavier.

Nothing about clear results means the losses were your doing. It means the panel they ran didn't have an answer on it.

Do you know what they tested?

Lead: Karyotypes for both of us, a thyroid check and a scan of my uterus. That was it.

Sonia: That's a reasonable starting set and it stops fairly early.

Was your thyroid antibodies checked, or only the TSH?

Lead: I genuinely don't know. They just said thyroid was fine.

Sonia: Worth finding out, and worth asking for the actual numbers instead of the word fine. Ranges vary and what's fine generally isn't always what's ideal in early pregnancy.

That's not me diagnosing anything, to be clear. It's the kind of question I'd want answered before accepting that nothing is findable.

Lead: Nobody has suggested asking for the numbers. What else would you look at?

Sonia: Inflammation, blood sugar regulation, nutrient status, how well you were sleeping and recovering, and your partner's side properly and not a single count.

Any of those can matter. I can't tell you which matters for you from a message, and I wouldn't want to.

Lead: I'd rather have someone actually look than be told to keep trying.

Sonia: Then let's look. My team will take the whole history on a call, including both losses, and tell you honestly whether this is something I can help with.

I should say plainly that it's a paid program. Would that be workable?

Lead: Yes. We're ready to spend on something that isn't another wait-and-see.

Sonia: Pick whatever time suits you.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 2: three losses, being refused further testing

Lead: Three miscarriages and my GP still says it's bad luck and won't refer me anywhere. I don't know how to argue with that.

Sonia: Three is past the threshold where recurrent loss is normally investigated in most systems, so that refusal is worth pushing on, not accepting.

Do you know what, if anything, was checked after the second one?

Lead: Nothing. Nobody has run a single test.

Sonia: Then you're not asking for something unreasonable, you're asking for the standard pathway.

Go back and use the words recurrent pregnancy loss, say it is your third, and ask specifically to be referred to a recurrent miscarriage clinic. Vague requests get vague answers.

Lead: I've been too upset in appointments to be that clear.

Sonia: Most people are. Write those three things down and read them out if you need to. It isn't dramatic, it's practical.

Lead: And if they still say no?

Sonia: Then ask for the refusal in writing. That question alone usually changes the conversation.

Lead: Would you be able to help alongside that?

Sonia: Possibly, and I'd want you in that clinic either way. What I do sits next to that investigation, not instead of it.

Get the referral moving first, then message me and we'll talk properly.

Lead: That's a plan. Thank you.

Sonia: Go and make the appointment.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

Sonia: Recurrent miscarriage is usually driven by inflammation and an under-supported luteal phase. Book a call and I'll show you what to change.

It invents a cause from nothing, presents it as established, and turns a woman's grief into a booking prompt.
